 feature within 'Áreas de Trabalho' (work areas), specifically for the purpose of organizing cables [Data: Relationships (62)]. This application is recurrent due to the inherent benefits of Canaletas in maintaining order and preventing the formation of cable tangles. The use of Canaletas directly contributes to a more organized, safe, and efficient working environment. This practice is well-established for managing wires and cables, including electrical, data, and audio, ensuring they are kept neat and protected [Data: Relationships (62)].\\n\\nThe presence of Canaletas in work areas is a testament to the importance of structured cable management in professional settings. It reflects an understanding that organized cabling is not just an aesthetic concern but also a critical factor in operational efficiency and safety. The relationships clearly define Canaletas as a tool for managing the physical infrastructure of a workspace, specifically addressing the often-complex network of cables that support modern office technology [Data: Entities (46), Relationships (62)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Rota Perimetral leverages Canaleta for structured cable routing\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 'Rota Perimetral' (Perimeter Route) is a cabling methodology that prominently features the use of 'Canaletas' (channels or conduits) for routing cables [Data: Relationships (1438)]. This integration is crucial for ensuring that cables are systematically organized, adequately protected, and efficiently managed along the perimeter of a given area or structure. The combined implementation of Rota Perimetral and Canaletas is a standard practice aimed at establishing and maintaining a tidy and functional cable infrastructure [Data: Entities (942), Relationships (1438)].\\n\\nThis approach suggests a strategic method for managing cable runs, particularly in environments where neatness and accessibility are paramount. By routing cables along the perimeter usi